      "text": "The initial web search provided some background on Ama Owusu and initial reception for *Glassy-eyed*:\n\n**Author Background (Ama Owusu):**\n*   Born in Kumasi, Ghana, in 1990.\n*   Educated at the University of Ghana (BA, English and Linguistics) and the University of East Anglia (MFA in Creative Writing).\n*   Previous short fiction appeared in *Granta*, *Jalada Africa*, and *Transition Magazine*.\n*   *Glassy-eyed* (Cassava Press, 2023) is her debut novel.\n\n**Book Reception & Context:**\n*   The novel was **longlisted for the Etisalat Prize for Literature**.\n*   In an interview, Owusu mentioned the novel was inspired by a glass workshop she grew up near in Kumasi.\n*   The work is described as a **multigenerational story** centered on a glass-blower's workshop, set in contemporary Accra.\n\nThe `summarize_source` tool confirmed the novel's details:\n\n*   **Title:** *Glassy-eyed*\n*   **Author:** Ama Owusu\n*   **Year:** 2023\n*   **Publisher:** Cassava Press\n*   **Summary:** It follows three generations of the Boateng-Asante family through the lens of a glass-blower's workshop, exploring themes of inheritance, craft, and economic transformation in Accra.\n*   **Key Themes:** Multigenerational family, inheritance and craft, Accra and Ghanaian modernity, structural adjustment, glass-blowing as metaphor, women and economic precarity.\n\nThe response from the Etisalat Prize longlist and the depth of the summary suggest a well-received literary debut focusing on craft and family history against the backdrop of a changing Ghanaian city.",
